(7684938, 1257882, 67145, '<td valign="top">many congratulations Lydia on your fourth place in this impressive challenge .. <br/>I love this so much .. <br/>and apparently different to everyone else .. i love the sunlight on the umbrella .. the man and the boy .. I feel it gives it more impact somehow .. and definitely more contrasts .. <br/>I don''t understand ppl''s desire for total ''reality'' in these obviously manipulated images  .. I like that things can be different from our reality .. and i''m blown away by ppl like you who create these stories with their art ...<br/> <br/>loved reading your story of how this all came together .. <br/>I can imagine the boy will want a print to hang on his wall .. and he''d say this really cool photographer wanted to use him as a model ... how cool .. sick .. mad and dope .. that covers all generations I think ..!!!! .. ;)</td>', 'many congratulations Lydia on your fourth place in this impressive challenge .. I love this so much .. and apparently different to everyone else .. i love the sunlight on the umbrella .. the man and the boy .. I feel it gives it more impact somehow .. and definitely more contrasts .. I don''t understand ppl''s desire for total ''reality'' in these obviously manipulated images  .. I like that things can be different from our reality .. and i''m blown away by ppl like you who create these stories with their art ... loved reading your story of how this all came together .. I can imagine the boy will want a print to hang on his wall .. and he''d say this really cool photographer wanted to use him as a model ... how cool .. sick .. mad and dope .. that covers all generations I think ..!!!! .. ;)', '2021-01-20 02:56:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(7684939, 1257708, 100393, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by willem:</b></div><hr/><i> Impressive, but underexposed and incorrect white balance. Applying a levels correction in photoshop with the white eyedropper tools makes this so much better. It retains the blue in the waterfall but makes the snow white. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I''m confident the white balance was correct here. This was the observed coloration of the scene. It''s a dilemma though to ''correct'' the scene or to convey the experience of being there. I have achieved the latter I think, is it a worse photograph as a result of that choice? Perhaps.</td>', 'Originally posted by willem: Impressive, but underexposed and incorrect white balance. Applying a levels correction in photoshop with the white eyedropper tools makes this so much better. It retains the blue in the waterfall but makes the snow white.   I''m confident the white balance was correct here. This was the observed coloration of the scene. It''s a dilemma though to ''correct'' the scene or to convey the experience of being there. I have achieved the latter I think, is it a worse photograph as a result of that choice? Perhaps.', '2021-01-20 06:50:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(7684940, 1258057, 114285, '<td valign="top">That is truly amazing. I knew you would end up entering a shot from your balcony. One of the main reasons I didnt enter.<br/> <br/>Congratulations.</td>', 'That is truly amazing. I knew you would end up entering a shot from your balcony. One of the main reasons I didnt enter. Congratulations.', '2021-01-20 07:41:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(7684949, 1257708, 4008, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Paul:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by willem:</b></div><hr/><i> Impressive, but underexposed and incorrect white balance. Applying a levels correction in photoshop with the white eyedropper tools makes this so much better. It retains the blue in the waterfall but makes the snow white. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I''m confident the white balance was correct here. This was the observed coloration of the scene. It''s a dilemma though to ''correct'' the scene or to convey the experience of being there. I have achieved the latter I think, is it a worse photograph as a result of that choice? Perhaps. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Not the intention to start a discussion here. It is your image, your memory and your choice. But just to illustrate what I meant, I have done a quick edit and loaded a before and after, here <a href="https://www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1258242" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/0-4999/4008/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1258242.jpg" xalt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/0-4999/4008/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1258242.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/0-4999/4008/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1258242.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''"/></a></td>', 'Originally posted by Paul: Originally posted by willem: Impressive, but underexposed and incorrect white balance. Applying a levels correction in photoshop with the white eyedropper tools makes this so much better. It retains the blue in the waterfall but makes the snow white.   I''m confident the white balance was correct here. This was the observed coloration of the scene. It''s a dilemma though to ''correct'' the scene or to convey the experience of being there. I have achieved the latter I think, is it a worse photograph as a result of that choice? Perhaps.   Not the intention to start a discussion here. It is your image, your memory and your choice. But just to illustrate what I meant, I have done a quick edit and loaded a before and after, here ', '2021-01-20 09:02:51.000000', NULL, 0),
